     "body": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/fcrepo/fcrepo-storage-ocfl/pull/61\" title=\"smart-link\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://github.com/fcrepo/fcrepo-storage-ocfl/pull/61</a> </p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/fcrepo/fcrepo/pull/2099\" title=\"smart-link\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://github.com/fcrepo/fcrepo/pull/2099</a> </p>\n\n<p>I’ve tried the PR with local filesystem binaries, and with fedora using s3 as its storage. There was a very significant performance improvement with s3 when dealing with a 100mb TIFF file and requesting the last 10 bytes of the file. There was no noticeable difference when requesting the first part of the file, or downloading the whole file of course:</p>\n\n<div class=\"preformatted panel\" style=\"border-width: 1px;\"><div class=\"preformattedContent panelContent\">\n<pre># Against aws 100mb file, download whole thing\ntime curl http://localhost:8080/rest/large_tiff -v -o /dev/null  -ufedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin \n\n100 96.2M  100 96.2M    0     0  12.4M      0  0:00:07  0:00:07 --:--:-- 14.2M\ncurl http://localhost:8080/rest/large_tiff -v -o /dev/null   0.06s user 0.14s system 2% cpu 7.738 total\n\n# Without range updates, get last 10bytes\ntime curl http://localhost:8080/rest/large_tiff -H\"Range: bytes=100924970-100924980\" -v -o /dev/null  -ufedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin\n\n100    10  100    10    0     0      1      0  0:00:10  0:00:08  0:00:02     2\ncurl http://localhost:8080/rest/large_tiff  -v -o /dev/null   0.01s user 0.01s system 0% cpu 8.058 total\n\n# WITH range updates, get last 10bytes\ntime curl http://localhost:8080/rest/large_tiff -H\"Range: bytes=100924970-100924980\" -v -o /dev/null  -ufedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin\n\n100    10  100    10    0     0     31      0 --:--:-- --:--:-- --:--:--    31\ncurl http://localhost:8080/rest/large_tiff  -v -o /dev/null   0.01s user 0.01s system 4% cpu 0.333 total</pre>\n</div></div>\n\n<p>With local storage, the retrieval times are already very short on my personal laptop, but there is some difference when getting the last 10 bytes. Without these changes it took 0.108s, and with these changes it took 0.030s. Getting the first 10 bytes took around 0.030 either way.</p>",
